<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canu Llywarch Hen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th–10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canu Llywarch Hen is a cycle of early Welsh poems in englyn metre, probably composed in the ninth or tenth century and preserved mainly in the Red Book of Hergest. In them the aged Llywarch Hen laments the sons he urged to their deaths in battle, and much of it is set in Powys. With the related <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canu-heledd">Canu Heledd</a> it ranks among the finest early Welsh poetry.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poems are the best known of the early Welsh cycles of englynion about heroes of post-Roman North Britain. They contemplate martial, masculine culture, fate and old age from a critical standpoint. In the poem Gwên and Llywarch, set during a watch at Rhodwydd Forlas, the speakers tell of a thin shield on the left side, of the horn with its band of gold that Urien gave Gwên, and of the boast that no old man was a weakling in youth.</p><p>As with the other saga englynion, above all Canu Urien and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canu-heledd">Canu Heledd</a>, it is uncertain how the poems were first performed. They are usually assumed to have accompanied a prose narrative to which they gave emotional depth, but this is not certain. In most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> they are preceded by the poem Claf Abercuawg, which the White Book of Rhydderch calls the englynion of Mabclaf son of Llywarch; modern scholars do not see it as originally linked to the cycle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Llywarch Hen may have been a historical figure: early Welsh royal genealogies place him in sixth-century north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> as a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urien-rheged">Urien Rheged</a>, the subject of the stylistically similar Canu Urien. The poems instead tie him to Powys, and it is generally accepted that later tradition moved his story there. The cycle may have been built up from little more than the genealogies, with his epithet inspiring his portrait as an old man. Their archaic style and occasional archaic spelling suggest that most were composed in Old Welsh and transmitted orally or in writing, and Jenny Rowland places the two poems on Gwên among the earliest saga englynion, older than some of those on his other sons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The chief witness is the late fourteenth-century Red Book of Hergest. The poems were also in the White Book of Rhydderch but were lost there through damage, and survive in two copies descended from it: Peniarth 111, made by John Jones of Gellillyfdy in 1607, and British Library Add. MS 31055, made by Thomas Wiliems in 1596. Dr John Davies of Mallwyd copied the cycle twice from lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> before 1631. All these descend from one lost original, which makes a critical edition fairly straightforward; Ifor Williams edited the poems in 1935 and Rowland in 1990.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Canu Llywarch Hen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The cycles of verse known as <b>Canu Llywarch Hen</b> and Canu Heledd, associated with Powys and probably composed in the ninth or tenth century, are among the finest early Welsh poetry and turned the defeats of the seventh century into lasting literature.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
